{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"brainy-cooked-hog","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\nAfter S1, persistence has a cohesive owner but `BindingStore` still embeds forward/reverse identity transitions, pending/confirmed state, numeric-ID re-key, comment identity, absence grace, tombstones, and unretire policy. Production rich-text convergence also reaches through shallow `all_bindings()` and mutates an inner dictionary, bypassing any future policy seam.\n\n## Outcome\nExtract lifecycle decisions into a private policy owner operating on the repository original dictionaries, with the public facade retaining every existing method/signature. Add a narrow facade operation for rich-text emission state. Preserve shallow `all_bindings()` compatibility, but make the narrow operation the supported production mutation seam for the later caller cutover.\n\n## Plan\n1. Move cohesive forward/reverse, pending/confirmed, immutable-ID re-key, comment-map, absence/retirement, tombstone, suppression, and unretire call clusters into `binding_lifecycle.py`.\n2. Keep the facade authoritative: it coordinates the lifecycle owner and repository unit-of-work; no adapter or reconciler caller receives a writable repository object.\n3. Use open typed views over the same dictionaries. Unknown and legacy fields remain present and writable only through named policy operations.\n4. Add `BindingStore.note_rich_emit(local_id, wire)` (or an equivalently narrow name) that preserves the current counter/hash behavior and missing-binding fail-open result. Do not change `all_bindings()` copy depth.\n5. Preserve exact current failure choices: confirmed 404 grace/reset, moved-key repair, retired corruption fail-open, live corruption fail-closed, append-only comment identity, tombstone suppression, and explicit unretire.\n\n## Testing (RED → GREEN)\n- Direct policy tests exercise both-index transitions and reload through the real repository after each state change.\n- Table-driven tests cover duplicate bind, stale reverse cleanup, missing/corrupt forward state, three-404 retirement, 200 reset, numeric-ID move, legacy no-ID behavior, tombstone suppression/unretire, and changed/unchanged comment IDs.\n- A narrow-operation test compares rich-emission hashes/counters and save behavior to the existing production sequence.\n- Signature and shallow-query regression tests pin the facade compatibility surface.\n\n## Compatibility / rollback\nFacade delegates can be moved back without changing bytes or callers. This slice adds the narrow mutation operation but does not yet modify RP-04-owned apply files. No lifecycle thresholds, corruption disposition, copy-depth, stored schema, or identity-marker writer changes.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Given valid and corrupt forward/reverse combinations, every lifecycle operation preserves at-most-one identity, removes stale reverse entries when required, and safety-aborts ambiguous double binds.\n- [ ] Three confirmed 404s retire, a successful read resets absence, immutable numeric-ID lookup re-keys instead of retiring, and legacy entries retain current behavior.\n- [ ] Tombstoned issues suppress recreate with an alert until explicit unretire; retired corruption and alert-write failures retain their current fail-open boundaries.\n- [ ] Comment IDs remain append-only/change-gated and old records without the field remain readable without rewrite.\n- [ ] The narrow rich-emission operation matches current hash/counter/save behavior while missing bindings remain nonfatal.\n- [ ] Existing facade signatures and shallow `all_bindings()` semantics remain unchanged; no production adapter obtains direct repository mutation access.","id":"fc1c-1b08-70c2-4afb","parent_id":"5e64-d9cd-2dd4-4036","priority":1,"tags":["rp02","reconciler","lifecycle","tdd"],"ticket_type":"story","title":"RP-02 S2 — Extract binding lifecycle policy and narrow mutation operations"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787022450388966001,"uuid":"02f17abd-2702-4c64-8a43-55728818f3e5"}